<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mikhail Glinka</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1804">1804</a>–<a href="/years/1857">1857</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Mikhail Ivanovich Glinka</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mikhail Ivanovich Glinka was the first Russian composer to gain wide recognition in his own country and is often regarded as the fountainhead of Russian classical music. Born at Novospasskoye in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a> in 1804, he studied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>, travelled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and studied composition in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> under Siegfried Dehn. His opera A Life for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a>, on the peasant hero Ivan Susanin, was a great success at its premiere in 1836, and Ruslan and Lyudmila, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-pushkin">Pushkin</a>, followed in 1842. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyotr-ilyich-tchaikovsky">Tchaikovsky</a> called his orchestral Kamarinskaya (1848) the acorn of later Russian symphonic music. Glinka died in Berlin in 1857 and influenced the composers known as The Five.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mikhail-glinka.jpg" alt="Mikhail Glinka" width="330" height="403" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: С акварели, приписываемой К. П. Брюллову · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Michail_Glinka_by_Brullov.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
